<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- NOTE TO EDITORS: never write a double hyphen anywhere in this comment.
	     It is illegal inside an XML comment, and while `plutil -lint` accepts
	     it, AMFI's parser (the one codesign actually uses) rejects the WHOLE
	     file with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error". The entitlement then
	     silently fails to apply and every plugin stops loading. That happened
	     here once already, caught only by signing a real build and looking.

	     Ainkrad loads Built-in App plugins as dynamic .bundle images from
	     ~/Library/Application Support/com.ainkrad.app/Documents/Plugins.
	     scripts/release.sh signs with the hardened runtime, which turns on
	     dyld LIBRARY VALIDATION: it refuses to load any Mach-O not signed by
	     the same Team ID as the host. Third party plugins are, by definition,
	     signed by someone else (and today are not signed at all), so without
	     this entitlement a correctly signed release loads ZERO plugins and the
	     app ships looking like it simply has no apps.

	     This is a deliberate, narrow trade: authenticity of plugin code is
	     enforced in process by `PluginSignaturePolicy` (see
	     Sources/AinkradHostRuntime/PluginSignaturePolicy.swift), which in
	     Release builds requires a valid Developer ID signature before
	     `Bundle.load()` is ever called. dyld's check is replaced, not dropped. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.ainkrad.app</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
